Croissants
6
Laminated yeast dough for croissants, with syrup and egg wash.
---
Laminated dough
wheat flour|150|g
fine salt|3|g
powdered sugar|15|g
fresh yeast|6|g
water|90|ml
soft butter (in dough)|15|g

Syrup
water|30|ml
powdered sugar|30|g

Glaze
egg yolk|15|g

Roll-in butter
soft butter|75|g
---
Make a well in the flour with the salt and sugar.
Dissolve the yeast in a little warm water and pour into the well.
Knead, adding the butter, to get a soft dough that holds together.
Leave the dough to proof about 1 hour in the fridge.

Roll the dough into a square twice the size of the roll-in butter.
Place the butter in the centre, corners of the butter square toward the sides of the dough square.
Fold the dough over in a cross to enclose the butter and form a neat square.
Even out the thickness by tapping with the rolling pin.

Do the first fold: roll into a long rectangle (length about 3× width, 1 cm thick).
Fold in three (single turn), roll again then fold in four (double turn).
Wrap in baking paper and rest 25 min in the fridge.
Do 1 single turn and 1 double turn in total (or 3 single, or 2 double), with rests between.

Roll the dough to 4 mm thick.
Cut triangles for croissants (or rectangles for pain au chocolat).
Roll up the triangles and place on a baking-paper-lined tray.
Leave to proof near a source of heat until well risen.

Preheat the oven to 220°C.
Make a syrup by bringing the water and sugar to the boil, then set aside.
Brush the croissants with egg wash (egg yolk mixed with a little water).
Bake about 15 min at 220°C, depending on size.
When they come out, brush the croissants with the syrup.
---
The same dough can be used for pain au chocolat (shape as rectangles with chocolate bars).
Respect resting times between folds for even layers.
Base temperature (ingredients + room) should give a dough around 23–25°C.
